      "content": "The impact of secularization on civic stability is complex and highly dependent on the specific cultural and economic context of a society, rather than following a universal trend. In regions where religious frameworks remain central to identity, their absence can indeed lead to significant social fragmentation, whereas in highly secularized democratic states, civil society often adapts through non-religious forms of association. Ultimately, policy outcomes depend less on the specific presence of religious institutions and more on the strength of the broader state capacity to provide public goods, education, and a baseline of social trust regardless of the source.",
